   navigation and text insertion to verify copying from the `<pre>`, pasting into
   the labeled textarea, importing twice, error retention, and mobile layout.
   `tests/persistence.py` also verifies imported contents across process restarts.
+
+## Live games
+
+- `POST /moderate/quizzes/:id/games` creates a new random-code game and redirects
+  to `/moderate/games/:code`. The existing HTTP Basic boundary protects the page,
+  JSON state, SSE, actions, and QR image. Quiz pages list previous games, each in
+  its own list item. Starting a game requires the existing moderator CSRF token.
+- Games snapshot the title and **ordered active questions** in a transaction.
+  Template edits/reordering/activation do not change an existing game. A new game
+  gets new players and answers. Deleting the parent quiz cascades to its games,
+  players, and answers. Export remains template-only.
+- State machine: `waiting → open → closed → open … → closed → ended`. Start on an
+  empty snapshot ends immediately. A version-checked moderator action prevents
+  double clicks, retries, and stale tabs from skipping questions. Answers and
+  transitions are serialized by the SQLite connection mutex; answer upsert and
+  revision increment commit together. Late/stale/out-of-range submissions cannot
+  alter a closed question or answer a different question.
+- The public join URL is `http://<request Host>/play/:code`; it intentionally uses
+  plain HTTP and accepts arbitrary hostnames. Its QR is generated locally as SVG
+  by the `qrcode` crate, using exactly the same URL helper. There is no QR API/CDN.
+- A random 256-bit, game-scoped, persistent HttpOnly/SameSite cookie identifies a
+  player. It is not an account. Reloads, browser restarts, and multiple tabs resume
+  the same identity; different games use different cookies. Duplicate display
+  names are allowed. Names are trimmed, limited to 80 characters, and escaped.
+  Player mutations require a separate HMAC-derived CSRF token; join also checks
+  Origin when supplied. No correctness data, future questions, other players'
+  tokens, or moderator secrets are in player HTML/JSON/SSE before Finish. Explicit
+  projections, not serialization of stored questions, enforce this boundary.
+- After Finish, option totals include every saved answer (one final selection per
+  player/question) and all correct choices are marked. Each phone gets its own
+  `Correct!`, `Wrong!`, or `No answer`. A closed game refuses all new joins, and
+  waiting join forms also receive the end state live.
+
+### Conference Wi-Fi and presence
+
+- One same-origin EventSource per page pushes a **full current public snapshot**
+  on connect/reconnect, so no replay log or fragile sequence of deltas is needed.
+  Per-game Tokio watch channels retain only the latest invalidation, coalesce
+  75 ms bursts, and never build an unbounded queue behind a slow phone. State is
+  sent only when visible data changed: another player's answer does not cause a
+  full payload to every phone. EventSource has a two-second reconnect delay;
+  application ping events and a 35-second client watchdog recover silent stalls.
+  Online/visibility/page lifecycle handlers support phone sleep and navigation.
+- Players are counted once across their connected tabs. Closing the last stream
+  removes them, without deleting identity or answers. Browser heartbeats renew a
+  45-second presence lease; abandoned TCP connections stop counting after that
+  lease (the stage checks at least every 10 seconds). Ping-event acknowledgements
+  also renew leases in background tabs. Presence is transient and re-established
+  after restart; all gameplay records remain in `storage/quizzy.sqlite3`.
+- The stage shows **saved answers / currently connected players**. A departing
+  player does not erase their saved answer, so the numerator can exceed the
+  denominator when answered players disconnect. Closed-question totals always
+  include those saved answers. There is no public roster or individual answers
+  disclosed to other players.
+- Phone taps are submitted immediately, serialized with a newest-pick queue, and
+  retried after network failures. “Saving your answer…” is distinct from “Answer
+  saved.” An old HTTP response cannot overwrite a newer game revision. Moderator
+  controls are not blindly retried; the client refreshes current state first.
+- All game responses are `no-store`; CSP and same-origin requests apply as on
+  editor pages. SIGINT/SIGTERM stops SSE streams before graceful server shutdown.
+  This is a single-process application; the in-memory hub is not multi-node pubsub.
+
+### Live verification
+
+- `tests/live_game.py`: HTTP lifecycle, CSRF/auth boundaries, hidden correctness and
+  future questions, inactive filtering, changing answers, no-answer/wrong/correct
+  feedback, stale transitions, closed answers, late/end joins, independent games,
+  and the zero-active-question edge case.
+- `tests/live_game_browser.py`: independent Chromium phone/moderator contexts,
+  QR rendering, exact labels, live counters/questions/results without navigation,
+  multiple tabs, remembered identity/answers, leaving/rejoining, late joins,
+  rapid choices, actual network-offline retry, end screens, and narrow layouts.
+- `tests/live_game_persistence.py`: offline `bin/serve`, shutdown with an SSE
+  connection open, immutable game snapshots, and persisted identity/answers and
+  open/closed/ended phases over server restarts.
+- Rust tests additionally exercise public projections, lease expiration,
+  multi-tab presence, and cascading cleanup. Existing CRUD/import/export/preview
+  browser and HTTP suites continue to pass.
